#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125811 :tutorial/content "In many real situations, a quantity increases quickly at first, but then the increase slows down and the quantity levels off near some fixed value.\n\nFor example, suppose a cold drink is taken out of a refrigerator and placed in a warm room. At first, its temperature rises rapidly because the difference between the drink's temperature and the room temperature is large. As the drink warms up, this difference decreases, and the warming slows down. Eventually, the temperature levels off and approaches the room temperature.\n\nThis type of behavior is described by an **inhibited growth model**.\n\nAn *inhibited growth equation* models situations where the rate at which a quantity $P$ increases depends on how far it is from a target value $a$, called the **equilibrium value**. This equilibrium value represents the level that the quantity approaches over time.\n\nAn inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = r(a-P),\n$$\n\nand an inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = r(a-P), \\qquad P(0) = P_0\n$$\n\nwhere $r > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $P < a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nIn this model, the equilibrium value $a$ acts as an upper bound. The solution increases over time but never rises above $a$, instead approaching it more and more slowly. The figure below illustrates this behavior.\n\n![](images/81/815441d6991917ade1594f1c60a90e9505577917b1088cae3b10c637b4486c15.png)\n\n**Note:** Inhibited growth and inhibited decay use the same basic idea: the rate of change is proportional to the difference between $P$ and the equilibrium value $a$. The only difference is whether the solution approaches $a$ from below (growth) or from above (decay).\n\nInhibited growth models can be written in expanded form. To identify an expanded equation as inhibited growth, we factorise out the *positive* coefficient of $P.$ For example, the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = 60-12P,\n$$\n\n*is* an inhibited growth equation for $P < 5$ and $t \\geq 0,$ since it can be expressed with $r=12$ and $a=5$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = 12(5-P).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9afedae3-a674-5a44-9d7a-4cdf57e66285" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4077 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152277 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151908} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe4c8bb4-b7c0-5afc-86fd-40c20df9b953" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Dependent and Linearly Independent Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152278 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151905} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152279 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151906} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151907} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153328 :tutorial/content "Suppose $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are differentiable functions defined on some interval $I.$ Then, the **Wronskian** is defined as \n$$\nW(y_1,y_2) = \t\n\t\t{\\left| \\begin{array}{cc}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\\prime } & {y}_{2}^{\\prime }\\end{array} \\right|}\t\n= y_1y_2'- y_2 y_1',\n$$\nand it has the following important properties:\n\nIf $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\nIf $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\n**Watch out!** We cannot deduce any information from $W = 0$ in cases where $y_1$ and $y_2$ are not known to be solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "ede0bd1e-1d97-5029-bbb3-a95bbadc7c88" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14497 :tutorial/title "The Wronskian"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153329 :tutorial/content "A **fundamental set of solutions** to a homogeneous, linear differential equation of degree $n$ is a set of precisely $n$ linearly independent solutions.\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''(x) - 4y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nUsing direct substitution, it's straightforward to show that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{-2x}$ are the solutions to the equation. \n\nLet's keep track of our solutions by putting them into a set $S,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\{e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the Wronskian of our solutions, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}){({e}^{-2x})}^{′}-{({e}^{2x})}^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not zero, the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. \n\nBecause the original equation is of degree two, and since the set $S$ contains *precisely* two linearly independent functions, we say that the set $S$ is a fundamental set of solutions to the differential equation.\n\n**Important:** The fundamental set of solutions is not unique. For example, the set $S',$ given by\n\n$$\nS' = \\left\\{4e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nis also a fundamental set of solutions. \n\nAs a general rule, we tend to pick the simplest set of linearly independent functions when constructing our fundamental set of solutions." :tutorial/id #uuid "e1733749-f7ac-5c62-80aa-f971e91c66d0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14498 :tutorial/title "Sets of Fundamental Solutions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153330 :tutorial/content "Suppose $\\{ f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\}$ is a set of functions defined on an interval $I\\subseteq R.$\n\n- The set is called **linearly dependent** on $I$ if there exist real numbers $k_1, k_2, \\ldots, k_n,$ *not all zero*, such that\n\n$$\nk_1 f_1(x) + k_2 f_2(x) + \\cdots + k_n f_n(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I.$  \n\nIntuitively, at least one of the functions can be written as a linear combination of the others.\n\n- The set is called **linearly independent** on $I$ if the *only* way the linear combination\n\n$$\nk_1 f_1(x) + k_2 f_2(x) + \\cdots + k_n f_n(x)\n$$\n\ncan equal $0$ for all $x \\in I$ is by taking\n\n$$\nk_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that these definitions closely parallel the notions of linear dependence and independence for vectors in a vector space.\n\nFor example, let's consider the set of functions \n\n$$\n\\{ \\sin x, \\, \\cos x, \\, 2\\sin x \\}\n$$\n\ndefined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nNotice that for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot \\sin x +\n0 \\cdot \\cos x +\n(-1) \\cdot 2\\sin x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since not all the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly dependent.\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "4d8e0478-c6f1-57b4-b41b-1fe464cb7fdc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14499 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166909 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166533} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74b8d3a6-0298-5f65-ad28-e2fc3cde731d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent to a Trajectory Given an Initial Value Problem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166910 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166534} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d219270-5202-54e6-8cba-dc816155bd7b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Nullclines of a System of Differential Equation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166911 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166535} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "129bee33-7c18-5450-affe-dd710a3a75dd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Trajectory Given a Starting Point and the Vector Field"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167994 :tutorial/content "Consider a system of two differential equations\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\boldsymbol{f}(x(t),y(t))\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}(t)={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $𝒇(x,y) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{f}_{1} ( x ( t ) , y ( t ) ) \\\\ {f}_{2} ( x ( t ) , y ( t ) )\\end{array} \\right]}$ is a vector-valued function of $x(t)$ and $y(t).$\n\nIn this context, the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the **phase plane** (or **phase space**) of the system.\nAt each time $t,$ the solution has a position\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x ( t ) \\\\ y ( t )\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\nwhich we view as a point $(x(t),y(t))$ in the phase plane. Now, as $t$ changes, the point $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ moves and traces out a curve in the phase plane.\nThis curve is called the **trajectory** (or **orbit**) of the solution.\n\nFor instance, consider the following (linear) system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=-4x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′} &=x(t)-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nA few trajectories (particular solutions) of the system are shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe17a02809800422e4f5cfa0d6a906a57ce1b9de06344092a28decea37e81e23.png)\n\nNotice that the derivative $\\mathbf{x}'(t)$ can be interpreted as the *tangent vector* to the trajectory at the point $\\mathbf{x}(t).$\nIn the diagram above, the (normalized) tangents to the trajectories are shown as small gray vectors. \n\nAt each point in the phase plane, the system assigns a unique tangent vector. Taken together, these tangent vectors form the system’s **vector field**.\n\nIn our example above, suppose a trajectory (solution) passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,-2).$ Then, substituting the coordinates of this point into the equations of our system, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =-4x+2y \\\\  & =-4(1)+2(-2) \\\\  & =-8 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x-5y \\\\  & =(1)-5(-2) \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent vector to the trajectory at $(x,y)=(1,-2)$ is\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}^{\\prime } \\\\ {y}^{\\prime }\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 8 \\\\ 11\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "0956c86b-1feb-53d8-b7bb-330bfed8dbe8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14424 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
